A||||31||SCCA FIERO||4.3 Vortec in a Fiero?||||14||
Z||000000||SCCA FIERO||07-14-2000||05:19 PM||||I just purchased an engine I couldn't pass up. It's a 94 4.3 Vortec with 84,000 miles. I got everything from the intake to oilpan to computer. Does anyone know of anybody that has this engine in a Fiero that I could get ahold of to talk to? This same engine is in my 94 S-10 ZR2 and I am pretty sure it will <B>smoke</B> my 2.8 Fiero. It get descent mileage and is rated at 190hp and 253 ft. lbs. I think it would be fun.<P>------------------<BR>-Chris <BR>88 Fiero Coupe<BR>V6 Fastback||||reg||14
Z||000001||Pontiaddict||07-14-2000||05:41 PM||||I haven't done it before but you should be able to adapt a v8 conversion kit to the 4.3. The front mounts will probably be different but everything from where the back 6 cylinders would be (on a v8)should be the same .You'll have more clearance for belt driven accessories and less weight than a v8 too.<P>GM got 450 hp on their Xtreme S-10 show truck . But that was with adapted v8 racing parts.(18deg heads, custom intake, stuff like that.) I don't know if they make those parts in the aftermarket though. I'd go with something lighter, but since you have the motor...||||reg||1
Z||000002||Terrybogin||07-14-2000||10:28 PM||||I have also thought about doing this same swap. I had a Blazer with the Vortec and it was very impressive. But since I would have to buy stuff from v8Archie and he doesnt want any of my money, I will keep my little v6.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||07-15-2000||12:50 AM||||SCCA...<BR>I've been thinking about this swap, too. I've got a 92 Sonoma with the 160 HP 4.3. Even though it's the TBI version and not the CPI, it's <I>still</I> a torque monster. Would really make the Fiero move. <BR>Has yours got the balance shaft? Mine doesn't. Vibrates like hell. <BR>I was thinking that Archie's kit would be the best option for the swap (Keep your same axles, and don't cut the wheel well). I wrote to him, asking for some info or guidance. His reply was something to the effect of "Yeah. A lot of guys have used my kit for 4.3s".<BR>No details at all. <BR>Oh well. Must have all the work he can handle.<BR>Good luck, if you attempt this swap. Please share the details. Inquiring minds want to know.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P>------------------<BR><BR>Raydar<BR>88 Formula T-Top <BR>88 Coupe (ISO 3800)<P>"Some mornings, it just isn't worth<BR>chewing through the restraints."||||reg||1
Z||000004||Patrick||07-15-2000||02:58 AM||||<BR>Geez Chris, you must really be anxious to tear up beautiful downtown Ogden... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>How would installing this engine affect the class you race your Fiero in?||||reg||14
Z||000005||olympic||07-15-2000||03:10 AM||||  I'd start out by just buying the V8 adapter plate.  Purchasing the whole kit would probably be a waste of money.  The 4.3 is a low rpm torque monster and would be one heck of a stop light racer.  Performance parts are not too scarce so 250 hp wouldn't be difficult.<P>------------------<BR>"Born free....taxed to death."||||reg||1
Z||000006||SCCA FIERO||07-15-2000||04:16 AM||||Put it this way... my S-10 ,with the exact same engine, has a locking rear diff. and it will roast both tires through 3rd gear (auto) and not even break a sweat! I gotta have it in my Fiero now!<P>My motors do have the balance shaft and the impressive intake. It idles very smooth. Once I forgot it was running because the radio was on and I turned the key :: <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">::<P>Hehe Patrick. The roads around here suck anyway... why not tear them up more?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P>Not sure what class I would be put in (maybe C or B mod?) but it would be worth it. I gotta have a rare Fiero. How many 4.3 fastback Coupes are around anyway?<P>I have been thinking of the hurdles I will have to overcome... in no paticular order.<P>1. I need an adapter plate... easy part.<P>2. This ECM is from an auto and I plan to use a manual. (Help Terryk)<P>c. How will this 4.3 mount to the cradle? How do they mount 350s? (help batboy)<P>IV. what's the proper way for a complete idiot to begin to splice the 2 wiring harnesses together?<P>E. What would be used for a fuel pump?<P>6. I have many different Fiero tansaxles to use. Which one should I use? Isuzu 5 speed, 5 speed Getrag or an M17 4 speed?<P>More to come...<P><P>------------------<BR>-Chris <BR>88 Fiero Coupe<BR>V6 Fastback||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||07-15-2000||03:05 PM||||There's a solid front motor mount (black steel plate) bolted in two places on both sides of the balancer (if you look close). The lower side attaches to the cradle. This pic is of a V8, the 4.3 won't be as long, but basically should mount similarly.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.v8archie.com/222.jpg"> <p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 07-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||SCCA FIERO||07-15-2000||06:27 PM||||Thanks bat.<P>I guess that mount is part of the kit used on that engine right? Since it's made for a V8 and my V6 is slightly shorter, I wonder if this mount would work. Maybe I would have to modify it to work or make one from scratch? Is that the only mount used for the motor? What about a dogbone of some sort?<P>I also have the serpentine belt, and since the PS pump won't be needed, what would be done with the slack of the belt that the PS pump takes up? Mount the pump there just to take up the slack?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||88formula||07-15-2000||08:46 PM||||Use a string to see if you can route a shorter Path bypassing the power steering pump. Bring the sized string and get a belt the same length as the string. I did this to bypass the AC with my Bonneville because the AC compressor seized and there is no bonneville without AC.||||reg||1
Z||000010||lowCG||07-15-2000||09:20 PM||||You can also take the pump apart and remove the vanes inside,and plug the inlet where the resevoir was so it's just become an idler.<BR>Don't forget to put oil in before plugging though!||||reg||1
Z||000011||BN Boomer||07-15-2000||11:52 PM||||Hey, why not use the power steering pump to power a hydraulic system so you can make your Fiero hop up and down? Wouldn't that be cool?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||SCCA FIERO||07-16-2000||12:14 AM||||HMMM!!!<P>------------------<BR>-Chris <BR>88 Fiero Coupe<BR>V6 Fastback  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/dancer[1].gif"> <p>[This message has been edited by SCCA FIERO (edited 07-16-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||batboy||07-16-2000||07:13 AM||||Obviously, if you use that same front lower V8 motor mount, it'd have to be modified to fit. If I ever do this project, that's what I'd planned to do (my Dad has a complete, but disassembled 4.3 in his shed that I probably could have for little of nothing).<P>The dogbone I have on my V8 bolts to a intake manifold bolt or two. This style might require the other end to be relocated on the trunk firewall, but since that's flat, it should be doable with a little reinforcing. Maybe check into that torque strut rod that V8Archie is doing now instead of the dogbone. The 4.3 has enough torque where you will need something to control movement.||||reg||1
Z||000014||SCCA FIERO||07-16-2000||02:37 PM||||I think I hear that 87SE screaming for a heart transplant to get back on the road. At the same time you could walk me through it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Monkeyman||07-16-2000||05:12 PM||||I'm not trying to take anything away from a 4.3 swap as I know absolutely nothing about motors, etc, but if this is such a good swap, how come the "big names" don't do it.  It must not be as popular as the SBC, 3.4, N*, etc.  I remember this being discussed a while ago, but is this a "new" swap?  Why isn't this done on a regular basis?  Cost?  Availability of speed parts?  Have they discontinued the motor?  Just curious.  ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Pontiaddict||07-16-2000||05:25 PM||||How do they mount the front of the 3.8's in the Fiero engine cradle? It might be easier to try that instead of modifying v8 mounts.<P>Monkeyman,Most people would rather do 3.8's than 4.3's for some reason. Also TBI 4.3's weren't the greatest motors and probably gave  Vortecs a bad name.<P>Hmmm, Didn't SLP prototype a Bravada with the  3.8's Eaton blower in it?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000017||Philphine||07-16-2000||05:42 PM||||i thought i heard that a 4.3 swap is like a v8 swap so if you're gonna bite off that kind of challenge might as well go for 8. i guess except for not having to go into the wheel well and maybe weight that would be true. also a 4.3 dosen't have that big an advantage over a 3.8 that is more easily adapted to the drivetrain. it's kind of caught in the middle.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Raydar||07-16-2000||07:07 PM||||I've heard that the 4.3 would provide similar performance to a 3800, with the ease of installation of a V-8.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> <BR>Also, AFAIK, there was never a 4.3 in a transverse FWD configuration.<BR>I have heard rumors of one existing 4.3 Fiero (in Canada someplace, I think). Have never seen anything beyond that, however.<BR>Still sounds like an interesting swap. <BR>Just requires someone willing to blaze the trail.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>BTW... be sure to use good mounts. I've already broken two left-side motor mounts in my Sonoma. (The 5 speed and the 235-60's didn't help any.)<P>------------------<P>Raydar<BR>88 Formula T-Top <BR>88 Coupe (ISO 3800)<P>"Some mornings, it just isn't worth<BR>chewing through the restraints."<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 07-16-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||batboy||07-16-2000||07:08 PM||||Philphine speaks wisely concerning the 4.3, it really is a good motor, just not an easy match up with our Fieros.<P>Pontiaddict, the 4.3 uses motor mounts like a V8 to fit between frame rails, where as ther 3800 is a front wheel drive engine that is designed to mount in a cradle.<P>SCCA, don't think I haven't dreamed about several engine swap ideas in order to get that dead 87 SE roadworthy. Look up some old topics where Squid was giving us some good info on that 4.3 Fiero project he was working on in the past.<P>I remember seeing a local Fiero club website once, where one of their club members had a 4.3 Fiero, but can't remember the URL. I was surfing on day last year when I saw it.||||reg||1
Z||000020||SCCA FIERO||07-16-2000||10:27 PM||||Went to Archie's site to look up what he has. The economy kit has the flywheel (is it the right one? will it work on the 4.3?) and is priced at $950. It has a starter mounting block (what's that and would it be needed on a 4.3?) <P>I was reading about his V8 FAQs and noticed that if you use a EFI V8 you will have to run the stock Fiero ECM and the ECM for the V8 as well. This is the stuff that I don't understand. How do these 2 ECM's work together and will I need to run both on the 4.3?<P>Maybe this is beyond me and I will get defeated by the complexity of this swap.||||reg||1
Z||000021||terryk||07-16-2000||11:59 PM||||I've never understood the logic of 2 ECM's. Maybe on a stretch where the trans is an E style, but that's a kludge in my opinion. Part of a good swap is picking the right ECM for the job.||||reg||13
Z||000022||batboy||07-17-2000||07:13 AM||||If I were doing it, I'd keep the Fiero ECM for instruments and things like that, but run a carb on the engine with a regular HEI distributor. Seemed like Squid talked about the flywheel situation in a past topic, but I don't remember exactly what is needed. I don't think you can use the V8 flywheel, but I'm not sure on that either.||||reg||1
