Z||000024||lowCG||07-17-2000||10:04 AM||||An update on using the PS pump as an idler;there's a wavy washer sandwiched in the rear cover that needs to come out for there to be some clearance,as well as removing the vanes.<BR>I think the flywheel is a Fiero unit with the center made to fit the Chevy.You'd then need a pretty radical Fiero clutch. ||||reg||1
Z||000025||Raydar||07-17-2000||01:18 PM||||SCCA... Do you have any idea if the 4.3 is neutral balanced or not?||||reg||1
Z||000026||SCCA FIERO||07-17-2000||07:08 PM||||I assume the crank is balanced by itself and the (in the case of my truck anyway) converter and flexpate were balanced together.||||reg||1
Z||000027||batboy||07-17-2000||07:12 PM||||Here's the scoop about 90 degree V6 (4.3) flywheels and cranks, straight from the Chevrolet Power book. All 1986 and newer cranks are designed for the one-piece rear seal. Earlier cranks used a two-piece rear seal. The flywheel (or flexplate) must match the crank. The earlier cranks are internally balanced and the flywheels are neutrally balanced. The torsional damper is used for these engines. The 1986 and later cranks are externally balanced and require counterweighted flywheels for proper balance. Flywheels and flexplates are interchangeable between the 4.3 and small block V8.||||reg||1
Z||000028||mshill||07-18-2000||02:26 AM||||Well, the maintenance on the 4.3 is easier than on the 2.8.  This evening I replaced the water pump on my 94 GMC Safari in just under 2.5 hours from start to finish.  The thing only has four bolts in it.  Zip, Zip... and it's off. Went on almost as fast.  What took all the time was getting the intake and fan shroud out of the way.||||reg||14
Z||000029||BizzaroGT||07-18-2000||03:05 PM||||Batboy, when using a V8 adapter plate on the fiero transmission how is the extra thickness of the plate compensated for when mounting the clutch?  Also on V8 conversions is a V8 type clutch used with a V8 type flywheel or is a fiero clutch used on a V8 flywheel.  For the 4.3 swap is seems one could use a flywheel from an S-10 pickup or blazer with a manual transmission and then have the flywheel redrilled to use the fiero clutch.  Any thoughts on this would be greatly appreciated as I have been seriously considering doing this swap for several months now.  The information found on this forum is fantastic, thank you to all of the members.  ||||reg||5
Z||000030||lowCG||07-18-2000||09:14 PM||||I saw something in a catalog that looked interesting;it was a kit to adapt a 60 degree V6 to a turbo 350 tranny.If it would be possible to use the thing in reverse,you'd be set,would just need to make a custom flywheel.<BR>The company is Speedway Motors (402-323-3200),and the part # of the kit is 973-18927,$139.95.||||reg||1
Z||000031||rockcrawl||07-18-2000||09:47 PM||||Hi,<BR> My $.02....<BR> The 4.3 is not commonly used because, as earlier stated, it has all the requirements of a v8 swap, so why not just... The front motor mount in the picture above would be my first choice. It is somebodys copy of an original GM part (big block p van I think). I used one on a 350 Lambo/Fiero. I have another one, but I am not willing to part with it. It would require that the mounting pad on the subframe be modified for a 4.3. In my opinion, a cast iron v8 is way too heavy for the fiero, so the 4.3 would be a good alternative, although it's probably also a little too heavy. <P> There is absolutely no reason to run two ECMs. The fiero ECM does not control any of the guages or indicators, except the service engine light. If swapping an ECM from a manual car into an auto Fiero, torque converter lock-up control will be lost, but there are far better ways to control it than by lugging around two computers and lots of extra wires.<P> Later,<BR>Jon<P>------------------<BR>'86 GT 3800<BR>'87 GT<p>[This message has been edited by rockcrawl (edited 07-18-2000).]||||reg||3
